    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d423">

                <pb facs="0522" n="434" sameAs="frus:frus1904/pg434"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr.
                            Takahira</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1904-04-12"><hi rendition="#italic">April 12,
                        1904</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 130.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Upon the receipt of your note of the 9th
                    instant the Department at once instructed Mr. McCormick, at St. Petersburg, to
                    use his good offices in behalf of the members of the crew and the five
                    passengers of the press boat <hi rendition="#italic">Hanyei Maru.</hi></p>
                <p>I am to-day in receipt of a telegram from Mr. McCormick, stating that the Russian
                    minister for foreign affairs has submitted this case to Admiral Alexieff by
                    wire, from whom he has also again asked for a reply to the request for
                    permission for a neutral ship to visit Khorsakov to take off the Japanese
                    consular staff and subjects.</p>
                <p>Mr. McCormick adds that he is using great efforts on behalf of the Japanese
                    subjects at Chita and hopes to make a favorable report with regard to them
                    within a few days.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
